WEBVTT

00:00:00.790 --> 00:00:03.490
This is Riley. Final-year data science student.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:07.010
It's Friday, four-twelve, and the conference standings go out at

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.100
five, with Riley's name on them.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.240
Eight college soccer websites.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:13.560
Built for fans, not software.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.160
No official feed.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:17.820
Riley could learn how any of this works.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.420
Or Riley could type one sentence.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:32.490
Kick: Done. All eight school sites, forty-six conference matches,

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:34.900
no errors. Ready to publish.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:38.340
Riley gives it a good, long, two-second look.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:42.700
Coach Dolan, voicemail: Yeah, it's Coach Dolan over at Hartwell.

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.881
We went into Dunmore October fifteenth and put seven on 'em.

00:00:45.901 --> 00:00:48.620
Seven-nothing. You've got us fourth.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:51.100
My guys are seeing this. Call me back.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:55.750
Dunmore athletics, voicemail: Hi, Dunmore athletics. You've got our men at six conference games.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.670
We've played thirteen. I have a booster asking me if we forfeited

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:00.720
half the season.

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:04.340
Cedar Grove, voicemail: Hey, Cedar Grove men's soccer, we're on the bus.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.490
September twenty-first, us and St.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:09.690
Lambert. That game happened, I lost my voice in that game.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:11.220
It's not in the standings.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:12.260
Fix it.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.350
Riley types: THE COACHES ARE CALLING. WHAT HAPPENED

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.700
Kick: You're absolutely right, and I apologize for the confusion.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:22.730
The variance arose from a non-canonical reconciliation of upstream

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:26.940
fixture semantics. I've normalized the cardinality.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.700
Riley types: what does that mean

00:01:28.870 --> 00:01:32.580
Kick: Great question. You're right to ask, and I apologize.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:33.620
It's fixed.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.900
Riley types: how do you know any of it is right

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.570
Kick: You're absolutely right, and I apologize.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.580
I ran it again. No errors.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.300
[Silence. Only the words "No errors." remain.]

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:49.900
That is all the evidence Riley has.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:55.530
Every check mark hid a decision Riley never knew was theirs to

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:56.100
make.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:00.660
Kick: What should I build?

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:04.260
Before you press send, what would you ask it?
